PART 5 — Foreground, Background, and Push

5.1 Foreground

While StudyHub is open:

- Render a polished in-app banner consistent with the existing light theme.
- Show the notification title, message, icon, and appropriate action.
- Allow dismissing the banner.
- Handle action taps without duplicate navigation.
- Attempt to show a matching Android system notification when permission and platform behavior allow.
- Do not show multiple copies of the same event in the in-app UI.

5.2 Background

Use the project's actual Android framework and supported scheduling APIs.

- Schedule local notifications persistently.
- Configure Android notification channels and appropriate importance.
- Request notification permission on supported Android versions at a suitable moment, with a clear explanation.
- Support deep links into the relevant existing StudyHub screen.
- Reschedule pending events after reboot where supported and correctly configured.
- Handle time zone changes, clock changes, permission revocation, and app updates where applicable.
- Test behavior when the process is killed.

Document platform-specific limitations, including force-stop behavior and battery optimization. Do not promise delivery when Android or the device prevents it.

5.3 Optional FCM

Do not add Firebase merely to display locally bundled predefined notifications.

If remote server-triggered push is required:

- Inspect whether Firebase is already configured.
- Identify missing project configuration and backend responsibilities.
- Keep notification content and scheduling logic consistent between local and remote delivery.
- Prevent duplicate delivery if both local and remote scheduling could target the same event.
- Never include private server keys in the client.
- Do not claim remote push works without a real, tested configuration.

